"The word 'sa'il' mentioned in the first verse means asking, inquiring, or flowing. There are three views regarding the occasion of revelation of this verse. According to the first, Nadr ibn al-Harith said, 'O Allah! If this is indeed the truth from You, then rain down stones upon us from the sky,' whereupon this verse was revealed. According to the second view, the polytheists said to one another, 'Ask Muhammad, for whom is this punishment?' According to the third view, the Prophet (peace be upon him) asked for swift punishment, and Allah commanded him to be patient. The first view is considered more sound.\n\nIn the second and third verses, it is emphasized that the punishment is for the disbelievers and that it comes from Allah. The word 'ma'arij' means instruments of ascent, stairs, or degrees. Commentators have interpreted this word in four ways: the first is the heavens and high degrees; the second is virtues and blessings; the third is ranks in Paradise; and the fourth is spiritual and immaterial ranks. According to this last interpretation, ma'arij refers to the ranks to which angels and souls ascend.\n\nIn the fourth verse, it is stated that the angels and the Spirit (Gabriel) ascend to Allah. This ascent takes place in a day the measure of which is fifty thousand years. Some scholars have said that this day is the Day of Resurrection, which will be long and distressing for the disbelievers and light for the believers. Here, by the Spirit, Gabriel (peace be upon him) is predominantly understood.\n\nBeginning with the fifth verse, gracious patience is commanded to the Prophet (peace be upon him). While the disbelievers consider the punishment distant, Allah sees it as near. On that Day, the sky will be like molten metal, and the mountains will be like tufts of colored wool. A person will abandon his closest relatives (his brother, his mother, and his father), and everyone will be preoccupied with their own plight. In the fifteenth verse, 'laza' (blazing fire), and in the sixteenth verse, 'shawa' (stripping away skins), are described as attributes of Hell.\n\nIn the twentieth and twenty-first verses, it is stated that man is 'halu'' (fretful): when evil touches him, he is fretful, and when good touches him, he is stingy and withholds good. Only the believers who perform prayer are exempted from this bad disposition. They are described with seven qualities: they are constant in their prayers; there is a recognized right (zakah) in their wealth; they affirm the Day of Recompense; they fear the punishment of their Lord; they guard their chastity; they are faithful to their trusts and covenants; and they are upright in their testimonies. These qualities render them worthy of Paradise.\n\nAlmighty Allah states, as the eighth characteristic of the believers, that 'they guard their prayers.' Commentators have explained the difference between being constant in prayer and guarding prayer as follows: the expression 'they are constant in their prayers' signifies never abandoning prayer at any time, whereas the expression 'they guard their prayers' signifies performing it in the most perfect manner by paying meticulous attention to what must be observed before, during, and after prayer. The matters before prayer are paying attention to the times, ablution, cleanliness, covering the 'awrah, seeking the qiblah, clean clothing and place, congregational prayer and mosques, and purifying the heart from misgivings to attain humility (khushu'). While performing prayer, one should contemplate that prayer is an ascension (mi'raj), and recite and remember Allah with presence of heart without turning right or left. The matters after prayer are abstaining from idle talk and sin. Alongside these, remaining ready for sentinel duties and jihad with the awareness that a secure Islamic homeland and society are necessary so that prayer does not become a 'fear prayer' and subject to enemy attack is also part of guarding prayer. Indeed, in Surah at-Tawbah (9:18), it is stated that only those who believe in Allah and the Last Day, establish prayer, give zakah, and fear none but Allah shall maintain the mosques. This eighth characteristic demonstrates that in Islam, social order, public security, governance, and military affairs are connected to the objective of guarding prayer. Even though the command to fight had not yet been revealed in Mecca, these principles were in the preparatory stage.\n\nIt is seen that the beginning and end of these eight qualities are framed by prayer, all of them are summarized as attributes of the person who performs prayer, and it is thus explained that prayer is the pillar of religion. In verse 35, by the statement 'These will be honored in gardens,' it is indicated that these eight traits correspond to the eight gates of Paradise.\n\nIn verses 36–39, the state of the deniers is described. The polytheists were gathering around the Prophet (peace be upon him) in circles and separate groups, mocking what he said and declaring, 'If these people enter Paradise as Muhammad says, we shall enter before them.' Thereupon, these verses were revealed. The expression 'in separate groups' means divided and scattered, each belonging to a faction. Allah asks whether each one of them expects to be admitted into a Garden of Delight; nay, He states that He created them from what they know (from a sperm-drop).\n\nIn verses 40–44, Allah swears by the Lord of the easts and the wests, declaring that He is able to replace them with others better than them, and that no one can outstrip Him. So let them converse vainly and amuse themselves until they meet their Day which they are promised (the Day of Resurrection). On that Day, they will emerge swiftly from the graves, rushing as though they were racing toward idols, with their eyes cast down, disgrace covering them. That is the Day they were promised.\n\nSurah al-Ma'arij concludes with a warning, explaining that Allah has the power to change things belonging to the future, as described in the verse of Surah al-Haqqah (69:38–39): 'So I swear by what you see and what you do not see...' Surah Nuh will follow in order to explain this power through an example seen in the past. The word translated as 'separate groups' in the verses is the plural of 'izah' and derives from the root 'azw,' meaning 'to be affiliated with.' These warnings against the mocking attitude of the polytheists serve as a reminder of Allah's omnipotence and the fate of the deniers."